  "reply_to_user": "Hi Sofia, sorry you're stuck offline. Our system shows your laptop isn't connecting to the internet at all right now, so let's narrow down where the problem is. Please try these steps in order:\n1. Check if your phone or another device on the same home Wi-Fi can load websites. If nothing works on any device, the problem is your home internet, not your laptop.\n2. Turn Wi-Fi off and back on, or choose to forget your home network and reconnect with your Wi-Fi password.\n3. Restart your laptop.\n4. Restart your home router: unplug it for about 30 seconds, plug it back in, and wait a few minutes for it to come back up.\n5. If websites still don't load, connect your laptop to your phone's hotspot or plug it into the router with a cable. If that works, the laptop is fine and the problem is your Wi-Fi.\nOnce you're back online, please stay signed in to your laptop for about 30 minutes. It hasn't checked in with our systems for a while, and this gives it time to pick up pending updates. Then reply and tell us: (a) whether your other devices work on your Wi-Fi, and (b) which step fixed it, or that nothing did. If nothing works, we'll pass this to our next-level team.",
  "internal_note": "User: Sofia Rossi (id 7). Account active, not blocked, VPN access enabled, 0 failed logins. Device BL-LT-0107 (Win 11): online=false, last check-in 2026-09-29, warning 'No check-in for 12 days'. Being offline fits the reported symptom (KB-014 quick check 3). Because it's been more than 7 days without a check-in, KB-018 follow-up also applies. Sent the user the KB-014 fix steps, asked whether other devices on her Wi-Fi work, and asked her to stay online 30 min so the device checks in (KB-018). Next steps: once she replies, re-run check_device_status to confirm a fresh check-in. Escalate to Tier 2 if the laptop can't get online even on hotspot or wired (points to a laptop network fault), or if it's online but still not checking in after a restart (KB-018). If public sites work but internal ones don't, follow KB-016.",
